From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] iommu: sun60i: program the block again on resume

The driver had no PM callbacks, and everything it writes into the block
lives only in the registers: ENABLE (+0x014), TTB (+0x020), TLB_PREFETCH
(+0x02c), the two invalidation modes (+0x040, +0x060), INT_ENABLE (+0x080),
and per master with a user BYP (+0x404 + 0x200 * m) and INT_ENABLE (+0x30 of
the same block). If the block loses its state -- a system suspend that
removes its power, or a domain that is ever allowed to drop -- the display
engine comes back with BYP at its reset value 1 and scans an IOVA as a
physical address: no fault, a garbage screen, and every register reading
plausibly. Reset values as read on the 8 GB board: ENABLE 0, TLB_PREFETCH 0x00030000, BYP 1.

WHAT CHANGES

A resume_early callback that programs the block again from the driver's own
state, the way attach does: sun60i_iommu_hw_enable() with the current
domain's L1 table (TTB, prefetch, both invalidation modes, INT_ENABLE, the
flush in the flush_all() order, ENABLE), then each master with a user: INT_ENABLE,
its micro TLB flush, BYP 0. That master sequence moves out of
sun60i_iommu_master_translate() into sun60i_iommu_master_program(), so attach
and resume share it; attach still takes the port gate first. With no domain
every master is in bypass, which is the reset state, and resume writes
nothing. The callback logs what it found and what it did, at info level
until the hardware proof exists.

  - Nothing is saved and there is no suspend callback: every value follows
    from iommu->domain and iommu->users[], and the page tables are in DRAM,
    which keeps its contents across a suspend.
  - No clock is touched on either side. The register interface needs
    SYS_MCLK, and an access with it gated stalls the bus (measured
    2026-09-22);
    the driver enables it at probe and never gates it. A master's MBUS port
    gate is held while the master has a user, so every master block
    written here answers. Masters without a user are not touched.
  - resume_early, not resume_noirq. The noirq phase is where genpd powers a
    gateable domain back on, from the consumer's own noirq callback, and
    there is no device link between an IOMMU and its consumers: "iommus" is
    an optional supplier (drivers/of/property.c, of_supplier_bindings), and
    of_link_property() skips optional ones unless fw_devlink.strict is set.
    Every early callback runs after every noirq callback and before any
    device's normal resume. mtk_iommu uses the late/early pair too
    (SET_LATE_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS); ipmmu-vmsa has the same no-save design
    (ipmmu_resume_noirq(), from its own context state) in the noirq phase.

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES

The BSP has a sunxi_iommu_suspend; its stack was reported to take a NULL
dereference there. Its code was not read for this patch, and nothing is
taken from it.

NOT TAKEN

  - Saving registers on suspend: they would have to be read back from master
    blocks, and the driver knows every value already.
  - Switching translation off on suspend: the display engine driver has no PM
    callbacks and keeps scanning through a system suspend.
  - Runtime PM: the block has no power domain of its own in the device tree,
    its clocks stay on for the life of the device, and gating SYS_MCLK would
    have to be ordered against every register access, including the fault
    handler's, to avoid the bus stall. No gain has been measured.

VERIFIED HERE (2026-09-25, not on the board)

Applies at fuzz 0 in the series. compile-check (A733_TEST_KERNEL=1) OK;
drivers/iommu/sun60i-iommu.o builds with W=1 and no warning, with the test
options on and off (native gcc 14.2). The object carries
sun60i_iommu_resume_early.

FALSIFIER, with a test-only patch (iommu-sun60i-lose-state-across-system-sleep, not carried) and
pm_test = platform: without this patch, the regs file of IOMMU1 reads ENABLE
0, TTB 0 and master 2 BYP 1 after the resume, and the display shows garbage;
with it, the regs read what they read before the suspend (ENABLE 0x1, the
same TTB, master 2 BYP 0 INT_ENABLE 0x1, INT_ENABLE 0x3) and the log carries
"resume: found ENABLE 0 TTB 0; programmed again, masters 0x4". One regs read
after the resume that differs from before refutes it.

Assisted-by: Claude

--- a/drivers/iommu/sun60i-iommu.c
+++ b/drivers/iommu/sun60i-iommu.c
@@ -427,6 +427,21 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * Program master @m's block to translate: interrupts on, micro TLB flushed,
+ * bypass off. Its port gate must be held.
+ */
+static void sun60i_iommu_master_program(struct sun60i_iommu *iommu,
+					unsigned int m)
+{
+	lockdep_assert_held(&iommu->lock);
+
+	sun60i_mwrite(iommu, m, SUN60I_IOMMU_M_INT_ENABLE, 1);
+	sun60i_iommu_cmd(iommu, SUN60I_IOMMU_MASTER_BASE(m) +
+			 SUN60I_IOMMU_M_TLB_FLUSH, BIT(0));
+	sun60i_mwrite(iommu, m, SUN60I_IOMMU_M_BYP, 0);
+}
+
+/*
  * The first user of master @m: take its port gate, then program its block.
  * The gate stays enabled until sun60i_iommu_master_bypass(), so the block is
  * never gated while it holds this driver's state -- whether it would keep
@@ -445,10 +460,7 @@
 		return ret;
 	}
 
-	sun60i_mwrite(iommu, m, SUN60I_IOMMU_M_INT_ENABLE, 1);
-	sun60i_iommu_cmd(iommu, SUN60I_IOMMU_MASTER_BASE(m) +
-			 SUN60I_IOMMU_M_TLB_FLUSH, BIT(0));
-	sun60i_mwrite(iommu, m, SUN60I_IOMMU_M_BYP, 0);
+	sun60i_iommu_master_program(iommu, m);
 
 	dev_dbg(iommu->dev, "master %u translating\n", m);
 	return 0;
@@ -1163,6 +1175,64 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * System sleep. Nothing is saved: everything this driver writes into the
+ * block follows from its own state -- the domain's L1 table, the masters
+ * with a user -- and the tables themselves are in DRAM, which keeps its
+ * contents across a suspend. So there is no suspend callback, and resume
+ * programs the block again from that state, exactly as attach does:
+ * sun60i_iommu_hw_enable(), then each master with a user. A block that kept
+ * its state is programmed with the values it already holds.
+ *
+ * The clocks are not touched on either side. The register interface needs
+ * SYS_MCLK, and an access with it gated stalls the bus; the driver enables it
+ * at probe and never gates it, and a master's port gate stays held for as
+ * long as the master has a user, so every block written here answers.
+ *
+ * resume_early, not resume_noirq: the noirq phase is where genpd powers a
+ * gateable domain back on, and it does so from the consumer's own noirq
+ * callback. With no device link between an IOMMU and its consumers ("iommus"
+ * is an optional supplier for fw_devlink), nothing orders that before this
+ * resume. Every early callback runs after every noirq one and before any
+ * consumer's normal resume, where DMA starts again.
+ */
+static int sun60i_iommu_resume_early(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct sun60i_iommu *iommu = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	bool translating;
+	unsigned long flags;
+	unsigned int m;
+	u32 enable, ttb, users;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&iommu->lock, flags);
+
+	enable = sun60i_read(iommu, SUN60I_IOMMU_ENABLE_REG);
+	ttb = sun60i_read(iommu, SUN60I_IOMMU_TTB_REG);
+	translating = iommu->domain;
+	users = sun60i_iommu_user_mask(iommu);
+
+	if (translating) {
+		sun60i_iommu_hw_enable(iommu, iommu->domain);
+		for (m = 0; m < SUN60I_IOMMU_MAX_MASTERS; m++)
+			if (iommu->users[m])
+				sun60i_iommu_master_program(iommu, m);
+	}
+
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&iommu->lock, flags);
+
+	/* Visible until HW-proven: diagnostics stay on by default. */
+	dev_info(dev, "resume: found ENABLE %#x TTB %#x; %s, masters %#x\n",
+		 enable, ttb,
+		 translating ? "programmed again" : "all in bypass, nothing to do",
+		 users);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct dev_pm_ops sun60i_iommu_pm_ops = {
+	LATE_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(NULL, sun60i_iommu_resume_early)
+};
+
 static const struct of_device_id sun60i_iommu_of_match[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-iommu" },
 	{ /* sentinel */ }
@@ -1174,6 +1244,7 @@
 	.driver = {
 		.name			= "sun60i-a733-iommu",
 		.of_match_table		= sun60i_iommu_of_match,
+		.pm			= pm_sleep_ptr(&sun60i_iommu_pm_ops),
 		.suppress_bind_attrs	= true,
 	},
 };
